<Layout title="First Steps After a Dismissal in Spain | López Márquez Abogados" description="First steps after dismissal in Spain: preserve the letter, note the exact date, keep the settlement and prepare the documents for an employment lawyer.">
<PageHero eyebrow="Resource" title="What to Do in the First Hours After a Dismissal" description="Start with the dismissal letter, the notification date and the documents already in your possession." primaryLabel="Contact the firm" primaryHref="/en/contact" secondaryLabel="More resources" secondaryHref="/en/resources" note="General information · Check the current official source before acting" />
<section class="section"><div class="container prose"><h2>Preserve the document and the date</h2><p>Keep the complete dismissal letter and record how and when you received it. The effective date and the notification date are not always the same.</p><h2>Keep the financial documents</h2><p>Keep the settlement, compensation proposal, recent payslips and any communication about the end of the employment relationship.</p><h2>Do not try to decide the legal classification alone</h2><p>You do not need to decide whether the dismissal is fair, unfair or void before seeking advice. The useful first step is to preserve the facts and dates.</p><p>For dismissal challenges, the general procedural deadline is 20 working days. The exact calculation should be checked against the date and circumstances of the case.</p><p><a href="https://www.boe.es/buscar/act.php?id=BOE-A-2011-15936#a103" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Check Article 103 of the Social Jurisdiction Act at the BOE →</a></p></div></section>
<CTA title="Need to check your specific situation?" text="Tell us your dates, status and what you are trying to achieve." label="Contact the firm" />
</Layout>
